    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Is the following force field conservative:
                <md>
                    \vec{F}=(y,x,z)?
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Find the work done by the force <m>F</m> in moving an object
                around the unit circle in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the work done by the force
                <md>
                    \vec{F}=(-y,x,z)
                </md>
                in moving an object from <m>(1,0,0)</m> to <m>(-1,0,\pi)</m>
                along the helix <md>{\vec{r}(t)=(\cos t,\sin t, t)}</md>.
                (Hint: <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> is the parameterization of the curve
                with <m>{x(t) = \cos t}</m>, <m>{y(t)=\sin t,}</m> <m>{z(t) = t}</m>.)
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Near the surface of the earth, we approximate the gravitational
                force on an object of mass <m>m</m> by
                <md>\vec{F}_{near}=-mg\vec{k}= (0,0,-mg)</md>.
                Further away from the surface of the earth, the gravitational
                force at position <m>\vec{r}= (x,y,z)</m> is
                <md>
                    \vec{F}_{far}=-\frac{C}{r^{3}}\vec{r},
                </md>
                where <m>C</m> is a constant and
                <m>r=|\vec{r}| = \sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}}</m>.
                Note: For the gravitational force further away from the surface
                of the earth, you will need to rewrite <m>\vec F_{far}</m> as a vector
                by expressing <m>r</m> and <m>\vec r</m> in terms of <m>x,y</m>
                and <m>z</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Show that both fields are conservative and find a potential for
                <m>\vec{F}_{near}</m>.  Bonus points if you can also find a potential for
                <m>\vec{F}_{far}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>



